---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Service",
  "name": "Programmatic SEO + GEO Consulting",
  "serviceType": "Search Engine Optimization Consulting",
  "provider": { "@type": "Person", "name": "Pravine Manivannan", "url": "https://pravinemani.com/" },
  "areaServed": "Worldwide",
  "description": "Programmatic SEO, structured-data schema, GEO and AI-search visibility, and local SEO. Built on Next.js + Vercel. CISNET went from 27 to 145 indexed pages.",
  "url": "https://pravinemani.com/services/seo/"
};
---

<Base title="Programmatic SEO + GEO Consulting · Pravine Mani" description="Programmatic SEO, structured data, GEO and AI-search visibility, and local SEO. Built on Next.js + Vercel. CISNET went from 27 to 145 indexed pages. Toronto consultancy." jsonLd={jsonLd}>

<section class="page-hero">
  <div class="breadcrumb">
    <a href="/services/" style="color: var(--text-dim);">services</a>
    <span style="color: var(--text-muted, #444);">/</span>
    <span class="you">seo + geo</span>
  </div>
  <h1>Programmatic SEO + GEO,<br><span class="accent">engineered for AI search.</span></h1>
  <p class="lead">Search visibility built on Next.js and structured data, not WordPress plugins. Pages generated at scale, schema for every page type, and a setup that lets AI search engines cite you. Migrations done without losing rankings.</p>
  <div class="cta-row">
    <a href="/work/case-studies/cisnet/" class="cta-btn secondary">See the CISNET case study</a>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="vert-grid">

    <article class="vert-card" id="pseo">
      <div class="vert-head"><span class="vert-id">01</span><h2>Programmatic SEO</h2></div>
      <p>For CISNET I generated 200+ pages from one template, one per certification and city. Indexed pages went from 27 to 145. Hundreds of search-targeted pages generated from one template plus your data: a page per city you serve, per product category, per use-case. Each page is real, indexable and competes on its own.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>A written content model: which combinations earn a page, and which are filler</li>
        <li>A Next.js build that generates pages and the sitemap as you add data</li>
        <li>Internal links between related pages so search authority flows</li>
        <li>Safeguards so Google doesn't flag the pages as thin</li>
      </ul>
    </article>

    <article class="vert-card" id="schema">
      <div class="vert-head"><span class="vert-id">02</span><h2>Schema + structured data</h2></div>
      <p>Markup that tells Google exactly what each page is: a course, a business, an FAQ, a product. It earns rich results in search (FAQ dropdowns, star ratings, business cards) and helps AI tools cite your content.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Rich-result eligibility for every page type you have (Course, LocalBusiness, FAQ, Article, Product, and more)</li>
        <li>Breadcrumbs Google can read, for the breadcrumb display in results</li>
        <li>FAQ and How-To markup only on pages that can win featured snippets</li>
        <li>Every page validated through Google's Rich Results Test before it ships</li>
      </ul>
    </article>

    <article class="vert-card" id="geo">
      <div class="vert-head"><span class="vert-id">03</span><h2>GEO / AI search</h2></div>
      <p>Your site set up to be cited by ChatGPT, Perplexity, Claude, Google AI Overviews and Bing Copilot, not just ranked in regular search. The rules differ: pages need to be quotable in one sentence.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>An llms.txt file that tells AI crawlers what to read first</li>
        <li>Content restructured so AI tools can lift clean one-sentence answers</li>
        <li>Crawler permissions set so the right AI tools reach your content</li>
        <li>Monthly tracking of which AI tools cite you and which cite competitors</li>
      </ul>
    </article>

    <article class="vert-card" id="local">
      <div class="vert-head"><span class="vert-id">04</span><h2>Local SEO</h2></div>
      <p>For businesses serving a specific city, neighbourhood or service area. Show up in Google Maps, land in the local 3-pack, and keep reviews coming.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Google Business Profile cleanup: categories, services, photos, hours and weekly posts</li>
        <li>Location pages that read like real content, not the doorway pages Google penalizes</li>
        <li>Directory cleanup across the listings that move local rank (Yelp, BBB, industry-specific)</li>
        <li>A review process that keeps your star count and review pace healthy</li>
      </ul>
    </article>

  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="section-head reveal">
    <div class="title-block">
      <div class="label">how it works</div>
      <h2>Engagement shapes for SEO work.</h2>
      <p>The same paid engagement shapes apply across every focus area. Full ladder at <a href="/services/">/services</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="vert-engagements">
    <a href="/services/#audit" class="vert-eng"><span class="lab">SEO + GEO AUDIT</span><span class="when">FIXED-SCOPE WEEK · QUOTE ON SCOPE</span></a>
    <a href="/services/#sprint" class="vert-eng"><span class="lab">PSEO SPRINT</span><span class="when">FIXED-SCOPE BUILD · QUOTE ON SCOPE</span></a>
    <a href="/services/#custom" class="vert-eng"><span class="lab">SITE REBUILD</span><span class="when">MULTI-WORKFLOW · MILESTONE-PAID</span></a>
    <a href="/services/#retainer" class="vert-eng"><span class="lab">OPS RETAINER</span><span class="when">MONTHLY · POST-ENGAGEMENT ONLY</span></a>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="vert-cta reveal" style="text-align: center;">
    <p style="margin: 0;">A migration or pSEO surface to scope? <a href="/contact/" style="color: var(--green); border-bottom: 1px dashed var(--green);">Send the brief →</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

</Base>
